doc Backend truck: translates Packenger packages to C, compiled with `cc -O2`.
  `ctables` is a second backend for the same front end: `--target ctables`
  fetches `ctables/<kind>` for every node kind instead of
  `bintables/<kind>`. Nothing in `std` or the compiler changes; the
  [toolchain](ctables/toolchain.md) package runs `cc` instead of `nasm` and
  `ld`.

  ```sh
  packenger run examples/fib.pkg --target ctables
  packenger asm examples/fib.pkg --target ctables    # print the C
  ```

  ## Conventions

  | what | how |
  |---|---|
  | every value | a `W` (`int64_t`), as in a bintables register |
  | expressions | C expressions; operands that must be evaluated in order go into temporaries in GCC statement expressions `({ ... })` |
  | order of evaluation | the same as bintables: operands left to right, call and syscall arguments right to left |
  | local variables | `W L[]`, one slot per variable |
  | parameters | `W p0, W p1, ...`, after an unused first parameter |
  | control flow | `goto` and the same labels as bintables |
  | memory layout | the same as bintables: strings, slices and structs are identical in memory |
  | integers | `-fwrapv`: overflow wraps; shift counts are taken modulo 64 |
  | runtime | `support` sections in C: allocation, printing, panics, `syscall` |

  Because `cc -O2` allocates registers and optimizes across statements,
  programs usually run faster than with bintables, which keeps every value
  on the stack. The price is a C compiler at build time and a dynamically
  linked executable.

  Statement expressions need GCC or Clang; `cc` must be one of them.

